# All places of ℚ̄· F_M have degree one

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.deg_eq_one_modularFunctionFieldBar`
(`462b7d63-c2fe-5723-b46e-ce8ae3eba2df`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `914c2faf-9973-5702-8ae3-82356f87970e`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $M$ be a positive integer. Write $F_M$ for `modularFunctionFieldFull M`, the subfield of the
Laurent series field $\mathbb Q((X))$ generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the set `divisorExpansions M`,
and let `modularFunctionFieldBar M` be its base change `laurentBaseChange`, namely the intermediate
field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((X))$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the image of $F_M$
under the coefficientwise embedding $\mathbb Q((X)) \to \overline{\mathbb Q}((X))$. The assertion is
that every place $w$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ has degree one. Here
a place consists of a valuation subring of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar M` which contains the
image of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, is not the whole field, and is a principal ideal ring; its degree
is the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-dimension of the residue field of that local ring. Thus the conclusion
says that the residue field at every such place is $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ itself, i.e. all places of
the base-changed modular function field are rational over the constant field.

This is the standard fact that over an algebraically closed constant field every place of a function
field has residue degree one, here for the function field of the modular curve of level $M$ after
base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. It supplies the degree-one hypothesis used throughout the
divisor-level Eichler–Shimura bookkeeping and the comparison of places of the characteristic-zero
model with those of its reductions.

